 (keyword index)←choices ParseSelectKeyword answer;valid;nums
⍝ Interprets `answer` as one of the keywords `Select` accepts, no matter whether it was entered by
⍝ the user or comes from `∆Automation`:
⍝  * "q" (for "quit") stands for `⍬`
⍝  * "a" (for "all") stands for all item numbers
⍝  * `a~{n}` (for "all but n") stands for all item numbers but n, where n may consist of numbers as well
⍝    as ranges like `3-6`, see `ParseSelectNumbers`\\
⍝ `keyword` is the keyword that was recognized, and `index` the item numbers it stands for.\\
⍝ In case `answer` is not one of these keywords, or the numbers after "a~" are invalid, an empty
⍝ `keyword` is returned; it's then up to the caller to decide what to do.\\
⍝ Note that this function checks none of `manyFlag`, `mustFlag` and `quitFlag`: that's the caller's business,
⍝ because the user and `∆Automation` are not treated the same in that respect.
 keyword←''
 index←⍬
 :If (,'q')≡,answer
     keyword←,'q'
 :ElseIf (,'a')≡,answer
     keyword←,'a'
     index←⍳≢choices
 :ElseIf 'a~'≡2↑answer
     (valid nums)←(≢choices)ParseSelectNumbers 2↓answer
     :If valid
         keyword←'a~'
         index←(⍳≢choices)~nums
     :EndIf
 :EndIf
⍝Done
